      "title": "Deploy the next anon.wei",
      "tagline": "Put anon.wei’s next version on Ethereum, its catch-up registry on three chains, and the first points: 21 transactions, from any wallet.",
      "about": [
        {
          "h": "What it does",
          "p": "anon.wei opens on a new device by reading the pool’s whole history, which takes a long time on a busy chain. The catch-up registry keeps the pool’s tree at some counts, each checked against the pool when it is posted, so a new device starts from the newest and reads the older history in the background. The next page also adds wallet apps through WalletConnect, and fixes."
        },
        {
          "h": "What gets deployed",
          "p": "The page’s 16 chunks on Ethereum (plain code the page is read from); the registry on Ethereum, Base and Robinhood Chain, at one address; and the first catch-up point on Base and on Robinhood Chain, where the history is longest. The page’s own contract and the anon.wei name are the steward’s to set afterwards."
        },
        {
          "h": "Who controls it",
          "p": "Nobody. The registry has no owner and no settings: anyone can post a point, and it keeps one only if the pool itself held that root at that count. The chunks are code with no owner."
        },
        {
          "h": "Who can send it",
          "p": "Anyone. Each address is fixed by its exact code, so the sender cannot change what lands there. A point is read from the chain’s keeper when it is sent, and the registry refuses one the pool did not hold."
        }
      ],
